00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.080
So I'm here to talk to
you about all the features

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.200
that my team has done in
the Android P release, which

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.390
has just shifted to a
developer preview a few months

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:05.430
ago specifically the features
relating to the enterprise

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.870
and work.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:12.480
So our team had three key
themes for the P release.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.860
The first one was related
to managed devices.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.430
So these are devices that are
under supervision and used

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.370
in a work environment or
perhaps an education environment

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:21.341
as well.

00:01:21.341 --> 00:01:22.965
These are things like
the work profile,

00:01:22.965 --> 00:01:25.770
if people are familiar with
that, and also fully managed

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:28.132
devices when the corporation
will only own the device

00:01:28.132 --> 00:01:30.090
and will kind of oversee
every operation that's

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.410
going on the device.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.020
And a key process
within the areas

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.210
is device provisioning--
how do we enroll devices

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.700
into management and supervision?

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.620
The second key area
that we focused on

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.270
is around dedicated devices.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.735
Now Android is open
source, very extensible.

00:01:44.735 --> 00:01:46.110
And we've seen
manufacturers that

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:47.640
are pushing Android
into all sorts

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.470
of weird and wacky places.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.210
These are devices that are
used for a single purpose,

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:55.230
so perhaps like an
airport kiosk or a device

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.840
that's used in the warehouse
for taking stock and taking

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.486
inventory.

00:01:59.486 --> 00:02:01.110
So we made a lot of
investments in this

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.443
to sort of mirror the work
that our partners were doing.

00:02:03.443 --> 00:02:05.910
And we'll talk about some of
the enhancements and features

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.770
we've done there with
a couple of demos.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.720
And then finally security--

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.740
security will be a
race that never ends.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.097
You know we'll continue
to invest here.

00:02:14.097 --> 00:02:15.930
What's nice about the
work that my team does

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:19.050
is that the feature requests
that we hear from enterprises

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.500
around enterprise-grade
security just

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.020
go into the operating system
and permeate out to consumers

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.420
shortly after that
almost immediately.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.170
So we'll talk about
some of the enterprise

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.145
features that are present
in the P release as well.

00:02:32.145 --> 00:02:34.200
So let's talk about
managed devices first.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.540
As I mentioned these
are types of devices

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.120
that are used by what we
call knowledge workers,

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.660
so people who work in an
office perhaps and want

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.300
to use either a device
that they bring themselves

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.300
into the office like a BYOD
type scenario or a device

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.700
that they're given
by their employer.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:54.630
So one of the big
investments that we made in P

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.370
was really in response
to user feedback.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:59.580
So back in Android
Lollipop, I believe,

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.584
we introduced the work profile.

00:03:01.584 --> 00:03:03.000
This is where you
have effectively

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.400
a separate user
running on the device.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.995
And you have very clear
segregation between your work

00:03:07.995 --> 00:03:10.600
apps and your personal apps.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.040
This is a type of work mode
that works particularly well

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.217
for BYOD.

00:03:15.217 --> 00:03:16.800
So if you imagine
in my case at Google

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.340
I own my own personal phone.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.760
But I want to get
access to my work email.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.830
I can add a work profile,
get access to all

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.480
my corporate applications.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.892
But my employer
has no visibility

00:03:26.892 --> 00:03:28.350
into any of the
personal activities

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.310
I'm doing on the device.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:33.122
So as I mentioned this has
been a feature of Android

00:03:33.122 --> 00:03:34.080
since Android Lollipop.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.970
And some of the feedback
we got was around

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:37.230
improving the user experience.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.330
There were some
challenges in this area.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:42.240
So firstly you'll notice on the
screen in the left-- it's just

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.800
a screenshot from
Oreo-- that all

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.510
of the work and
personal applications

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:46.770
are mixed together.

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:48.540
You can see that
there's a small badge

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.090
icon that's used to denote
the personal calendar

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:52.350
versus the calendar.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:53.940
So that is the
personal application

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.730
versus the work application.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:58.957
And we heard from users
it's hard to parse

00:03:58.957 --> 00:03:59.790
this duplicate list.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.610
You have this long
list of applications.

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:03.420
You have a personal
email and a work email.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.190
People didn't understand
the difference.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:08.160
And this bright orange briefcase
felt slightly disconnected

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:09.910
from the Android system UI.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:13.590
So we made some changes
to that around that in P.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.720
So firstly work and
personal applications

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:19.390
are separated into separate tabs
within the launcher themselves.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:21.390
So that's kind of an
inbuilt filter between work

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.880
and personal modes.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.000
You'll notice that the
briefcase icon is now

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.760
changed to a nice shade
of blue, more in line

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.440
with the overall
Android system UI, which

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.900
using hints and hints
of blue here and there.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.920
And you'll now notice that
we have a toggle right

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.380
up front and center
on the work tab,

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:43.309
giving users the ability to
turn off the work profile.

00:04:43.309 --> 00:04:45.850
So you imagine the use case here
is that it's Friday evening.

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:48.510
And I no longer want to
receive any notifications

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.110
with work chats or email.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:52.140
I can simply turn
off the work profile.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.090
Those apps are
instantly suspended.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:56.050
I no longer receive
notifications.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:58.299
And then on Monday morning
when I'm back in the office

00:04:58.299 --> 00:05:01.015
and I want to resume, I simply
turn that toggle back on.

00:05:01.015 --> 00:05:02.460
But you can also
see at the bottom

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:05.740
there's a clear
indication that this is

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.590
the remit of my organization.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:10.302
Their management only extends
as far as these applications.

00:05:10.302 --> 00:05:11.760
And they have no
sort of visibility

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.240
into what I'm doing
on my personal side.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.730
So just to expand
across this as well,

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:21.000
we've added some user
education to make people

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.800
aware of this new paradigm.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.800
So the first time the
launcher is opened you'll

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.690
see this blue tab
that informs that this

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:29.940
is the new home for
work applications.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.030
And as mentioned there
before, this toggle

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:35.850
to turn off work applications
is kind of up front and center

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.070
with some demarcation
as to say this

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:41.970
is where your organization's
remiss kind of extends to.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.940
So I want to switch to
a quick demo of this

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.820
over on the WolfVision
if possible.

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.905
So this is a device
running Android Oreo.

00:05:50.905 --> 00:05:53.140
Just unlock it here, quickly.

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:55.830
So you can see here that I
have got just a regular home

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:57.167
screen with a launcher.

00:05:57.167 --> 00:05:59.250
And when I open the home
screen, open my launcher,

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:01.590
you can see I have
duplicate Chrome icons,

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:04.330
I have duplicate contacts,
duplicate calendar, here,

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:05.820
there, and everywhere.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.730
So you can imagine that's quite
difficult for users to parse,

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.500
and you may have
accidental clicks

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.742
as the user could
select the wrong icon.

00:06:12.742 --> 00:06:14.700
The actual ability to
turn off the work profile

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.119
has been available
since its inception.

00:06:17.119 --> 00:06:19.160
It was actually buried up
here in Quick Settings.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:21.010
It's already two swipes
down there, and then

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:24.630
a further swipe to the
right to turn off Work Mode.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:26.460
At this point,
work notifications

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:27.654
have been suspended.

00:06:27.654 --> 00:06:29.070
And now, when you
open the launch,

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:30.990
you see these sort
of strange gray icons

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.812
everywhere, which
is not ideal at all.

00:06:33.812 --> 00:06:36.290
So let's compare that
to a device running

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:40.030
P. Just unlock this quickly.

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:41.710
Sorry.

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:44.370
So again, you know, the
familiar Android home screen.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:47.010
I can swipe up, and now I've
got Work and Personal tabs,

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:49.350
so all my work, my home
applications are here,

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:53.010
I've got my personal photos,
my personal email, et cetera--

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:54.930
no duplication of applications.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.300
You can see that
Android is continuing

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.382
to suggest applications
to me at the top,

00:06:59.382 --> 00:07:01.340
and that will go across
both Personal and Work.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:04.120
You can see there's the badged
icons up here at the top.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:06.600
But now in the work tab
I have everything kind

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:07.820
of grouped together neatly.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:10.140
There's no mix and
match of applications.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:11.640
And on a Friday
evening, when I want

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.200
to go home and
have a beer, I just

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:14.940
simply turn off the
Work applications,

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.199
and everything stops.

00:07:17.199 --> 00:07:18.990
And then switching it
back on, very simply,

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:21.160
back to the Work tab
on Monday morning,

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.490
and I have to unlock
again, sadly, but--

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:25.436
whoops.

00:07:25.436 --> 00:07:27.440
And all my work
applications restore.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:29.760
I can now start
getting emails, sadly.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:31.818
So if we could go back
to the slides, please.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:38.550
So the behavior I've just
described there is actually

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:40.470
part of the default
system launcher

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:43.080
that will launch with
Android P, but if you're

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.670
a developer of
your own launcher,

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:48.510
you too can sort of join in
in this experience as well.

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:51.130
There is an API to request
Quiet Mode enabled.

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:54.570
That's what's turning on and
turning off the Work profile.

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:57.582
And when that
action is initiated,

00:07:57.582 --> 00:07:59.040
there's a couple
of broadcasts that

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:00.498
go out to the system
itself to say,

00:08:00.498 --> 00:08:03.270
like, Work profile is available
and Work profile is now not

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:06.030
available, and the launchers
can use that to kind of change

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:09.840
the colors of icons, and
mark apps as disabled.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:13.020
Any application can query to
see if Work mode is enabled

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:16.926
by calling that API there,
is Quiet Mode enabled,

00:08:16.926 --> 00:08:19.050
and there's further
documentation at that shortlink

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:21.105
if anyone's really
into more details.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:27.060
So the next piece of feedback
we received from users, again

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:29.550
related to the sort of dual
persona of applications

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:31.740
that you have as a
result of Work profile,

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.750
quite a lot of users
have this muscle memory

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.007
of using the Account Switcher.

00:08:35.007 --> 00:08:36.840
If you have multiple
accounts on the device,

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:39.840
say a personal account
and a work account,

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:42.659
most users are sort of used
to expanding the sidebar menu

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:45.307
and toggling between
the two account views.

00:08:45.307 --> 00:08:46.890
With the Work profile,
because there's

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:50.610
such separation
between the two users,

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:52.360
previously, there
was no visibility

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:56.690
from the Personal side of the
Work user, and vice versa, too.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:59.040
So in Android P we've
introduced this ability

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.820
to be able to switch
between the same instance--

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:04.080
sorry, a different instance
of the same application

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:06.009
that kind of exists
in both profiles.

00:09:06.009 --> 00:09:08.550
So in this example, as you're
seeing with the animation here,

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:10.500
I've got the Google
Tasks app, and you

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:13.800
can see that I've got
both Personal and Work

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:15.000
tasks I need to perform.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.290
So on my personal side I've
got a dentist appointment,

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:19.440
et cetera, and
organizing my wedding.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:22.240
On my work side,
when I switch over,

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:25.170
you can see that I need
to submit my expenses

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:26.862
and cancel my
one-to-one meeting.

00:09:26.862 --> 00:09:28.320
Now previously,
the user would have

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.090
to return to the home screen,
find the other application,

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:32.770
re-launch the application.

00:09:32.770 --> 00:09:35.880
But thankfully from
Android P, any application

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:40.000
can develop support for this.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.270
So there are some critical
APIs to support this.

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:45.090
The first one is
getTargetUserProfiles.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.560
That will return a list of the
available user profiles that

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:48.410
were on the device.

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:50.284
Typically in a Work
profile, there would only

00:09:50.284 --> 00:09:52.770
be one additional user profile.

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.330
We provide some
icons that you can

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:57.850
use to facilitate
the sort of smooth UX

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:00.000
during the switching
process, and you can also

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.170
get a localized label to say
that this is the account you're

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:05.084
switching to.

00:10:05.084 --> 00:10:06.750
When the application
is ready to launch,

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:08.970
you simply call
startMainActivity,

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:11.610
and that will launch the
instance of the application

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:13.380
inside the other profile.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.810
And again, full documentation
at the shortlink.

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.060
But what's crucial about
this is it's not just limited

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:19.530
to Google applications.

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:22.260
Any application that
would potentially

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:24.870
reside both on the personal
side and the work side

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:26.880
can implement these
APIs, and you too

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.040
can kind of implement
this seamless switching

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:30.270
between the two instances.

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:35.899
QR provisioning-- so one
of the focuses for our team

00:10:35.899 --> 00:10:38.190
is how do you enroll the
device as quickly as possible.

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:40.680
So you can imagine I'm
an IT administrator,

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:43.740
I've got a crateful of devices
that I need to provision,

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:46.437
and give out to my employees
as soon as possible.

00:10:46.437 --> 00:10:48.520
And we support a number
of mechanisms around this.

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:51.520
There's NFC provisioning, there
is zero touch provisioning,

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:53.794
which was launched last year.

00:10:53.794 --> 00:10:55.960
And one of the methods we've
supported for some time

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:57.210
is QR provisioning.

00:10:57.210 --> 00:11:01.620
So this is where administrator
can generate a unique QR

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:04.620
code containing the details
of the Enterprise Mobility

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:07.830
Management solution the
enterprise is using,

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:10.060
and that begins the
enrollment process.

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:14.400
What's new in P is the ability
to include Wi-Fi configurations

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:15.690
within that code.

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:19.325
So historically, if
you wanted to add

00:11:19.325 --> 00:11:23.344
the Wi-Fi configurations, you'd
have to enter those manually.

00:11:23.344 --> 00:11:25.260
And secondly, what's
most important about this

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:27.870
is actually the QR code
reader itself is bundled

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:29.400
in the operating system.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:32.490
So historically if you wanted
to use QR code provisioning

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:34.500
to enroll your
devices, you'd have

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:37.050
to first connect to Wi-Fi,
entering manual credentials

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:41.500
to do so, download some QR code
library to start the process,

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:43.414
and then finally scan the code.

00:11:43.414 --> 00:11:45.330
Now the good thing about
this is it's actually

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:47.730
an improvement of 30
seconds per device,

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:50.820
because the QR code library
is included in the OS,

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:53.710
and you can actually bootstrap
connectivity straight away.

00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:55.340
So if you can imagine
30 seconds per,

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:57.090
and you've got 1,000
devices, that quickly

00:11:57.090 --> 00:11:59.850
becomes quite a
large time saving.

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:04.080
What's nice about the Wi-Fi
configurations is that if that

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:06.590
Wi-Fi access point
is within range,

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:09.090
the device just connects
immediately and the enrollment

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:10.170
process continues--

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:12.960
Setup Wizard just
continues to go through.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.520
We support a number of
different Wi-Fi credentials,

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:18.460
so things like the
name of the SSID,

00:12:18.460 --> 00:12:22.200
the password, any proxy
details you want to specify.

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:25.444
That all can be
encoded in the QR code.

00:12:25.444 --> 00:12:27.610
What you might notice that
certificates are missing.

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:29.151
So this is an early
piece of feedback

00:12:29.151 --> 00:12:31.515
we've got from partners
since presenting this,

00:12:31.515 --> 00:12:33.390
and that's something
we're looking to address

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:35.190
in the next Android release.

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:40.020
And there is full documentation
on that shortlink there.

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:42.260
So when we speak to IT
administrators and customers

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:45.380
that deploy Android devices
in the organization,

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:48.302
we get constantly asked
for additional hooks

00:12:48.302 --> 00:12:49.760
and additional
tools that they want

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:51.890
to configure on their devices.

00:12:51.890 --> 00:12:53.780
One of the gaps in
Android for some time

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:56.760
has been around the Access
Point Name, the cellular APN

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:00.260
that you need to add
to a device sometimes.

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:02.150
So if I am a large
corporation, and I've

00:13:02.150 --> 00:13:03.980
got a strategic
deal with a carrier,

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:07.196
and they may be given a
custom APN, historically that

00:13:07.196 --> 00:13:08.570
would have to be
a manual process

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:09.736
to add that into the device.

00:13:09.736 --> 00:13:13.052
But now we can do that over
the air through the EMM.

00:13:13.052 --> 00:13:15.260
And we've actually given
more control to the settings

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:17.600
there to the IT
administrator, particularly

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:19.200
around the time and time zone.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:21.740
So we've heard about all
sorts of abuse scenarios

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:24.290
where, if you imagine,
say I'm a truck driver,

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:26.750
and I've got an
Android device, I

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:29.220
may want to adjust the
time settings on my device

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:30.780
so that I appear
to be on a break

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:32.570
and I would know
when we push jobs.

00:13:32.570 --> 00:13:37.300
So suddenly we're clamping down
on that practice in Android P.

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:40.130
There's a full list
of methods in terms

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:41.810
of what we support
here, and more

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:43.400
in the linked documentation.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:46.070
So there's an ability to add
what we call an override APN--

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:49.340
that's a custom APN,
and then enable it.

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:51.870
There's APIs to set the
time and set the time

00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:54.890
zone, and a whole selection
of additional policies

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:57.710
that we've included
to kind of lock down

00:13:57.710 --> 00:13:59.330
certain aspects of the device.

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:02.090
That ranges from disallowing
Airplane Mode right the way

00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:04.630
through to disallowing printings
if your organization has

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:07.970
a problem with people printing
spurious amounts of paper,

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:10.190
we now have the policy for you.

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:15.950
Further features we
built into Android P

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:17.872
is the ability to
migrate the DPCs.

00:14:17.872 --> 00:14:19.580
So for those of you
who are not familiar,

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:23.090
a DPC, or a Device
Policy Controller,

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:26.210
is what the Enterprise
Management tool will actually

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:27.890
install on the
device, and that's

00:14:27.890 --> 00:14:29.990
the actual application
that's enforcing

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:32.270
all these different policies.

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:35.420
Sometimes it may be
appropriate for a customer

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:38.180
to migrate to a different
DPC, and there's

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:40.550
a couple of reasons
why that might happen.

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:42.650
So it could be
that my management

00:14:42.650 --> 00:14:46.250
provider is migrating from
an old stack to a new stack.

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:49.740
A new version of
their DPC is required.

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:50.630
So this could be--

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:53.690
we now have the APIs to
support that migration.

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:57.320
Secondly, the
management provider

00:14:57.320 --> 00:14:59.880
may be moved into the
Android Management API.

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:01.820
This is something we
launched last year,

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:04.790
and provides a much easier
way for management providers

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:07.430
to offer the full suite
of management services

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:08.940
on their device.

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:11.979
So again, that could be
another case for DPC migration.

00:15:11.979 --> 00:15:13.520
And then finally it
could be the case

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:15.950
that a customer is actually
switching from one management

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:18.931
provider to another, and they
need to install the new DPC,

00:15:18.931 --> 00:15:21.430
and transfer everything over,
and maintain all the policies.

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:25.310
They'll be no loss of
policy in this case.

00:15:25.310 --> 00:15:27.570
OK, so let's talk about
dedicated devices.

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:29.960
So as I mentioned,
these are the devices

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:31.820
that are often used
for a single purpose,

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:34.940
or maybe kind of a
ruggedized hardware that's

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:36.490
been augmented with
bar code scanners

00:15:36.490 --> 00:15:37.490
and all kinds of things.

00:15:37.490 --> 00:15:42.780
We see lots of innovation in
this space within our team.

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:46.700
So one of the most popular
modes for devices of this type

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:48.369
is something we call LockTask.

00:15:48.369 --> 00:15:49.910
So this is locking
the device to only

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:52.730
be able to be able to use
either one or a handful

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:54.170
of applications.

00:15:54.170 --> 00:15:55.880
So you can see on the
left-hand side here

00:15:55.880 --> 00:15:57.740
there's an example
where a device is being

00:15:57.740 --> 00:16:00.340
locked to be a point of sale.

00:16:00.340 --> 00:16:03.680
In the current experience,
LockTask mode only

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:05.430
supports a single application.

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:08.630
So you're only able to lock
to one application at a time.

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:10.640
That's certainly
not ideal, and what

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:12.470
we started to see with
some of our partners

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:14.450
were implementing
workarounds around this

00:16:14.450 --> 00:16:16.130
because locking
to one application

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:18.770
probably isn't
the best solution.

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:21.080
And what happened
was those workarounds

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:22.550
tend to be quite flaky.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:24.130
We would change
something in the OS,

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:26.129
and those workarounds
would simply stop working.

00:16:26.129 --> 00:16:27.740
So it was the right
time to invest

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:30.330
in a platform-wide solution.

00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:32.270
And then secondly, the
application developer

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:35.070
themselves, so in this case,
this Point of Sale application,

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:37.860
had to declare explicit
support for LockTask mode.

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:40.640
They had to include something
in the application that said,

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:42.440
I actually support
LockTask mode.

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:44.690
Now what happened here is,
because this wasn't perhaps

00:16:44.690 --> 00:16:47.270
the most--

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:48.890
people weren't
aware of this mode--

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:50.527
there's not a consumer feature--

00:16:50.527 --> 00:16:52.610
application developers
simply didn't include this,

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:55.610
and that severely limited the
catalog of applications that

00:16:55.610 --> 00:16:58.190
could be used in LockTask mode.

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:01.540
So anyway, as always,
good news comes in P.

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:04.025
So LockTask can now support
multiple applications,

00:17:04.025 --> 00:17:05.390
and we have a custom launcher.

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:10.119
So we can see here that I've
selected six applications,

00:17:10.119 --> 00:17:13.530
and the user is able
to use these things

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:16.250
and come in and out
of them as they wish.

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:18.510
Any application can be
put into LockTask mode.

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:20.131
So here I've got
Chrome and Maps.

00:17:20.131 --> 00:17:21.589
The Chrome and Maps
developers have

00:17:21.589 --> 00:17:24.950
had to do nothing to
support this LockTask mode.

00:17:24.950 --> 00:17:27.560
And then finally, we have more
flexibility around the launcher

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:29.360
in terms of what UI
elements are shown,

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:31.550
and the administrator has
full control around this.

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:34.717
So they can either choose
to show the Home button,

00:17:34.717 --> 00:17:36.800
they can choose that certain
settings are enabled,

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:38.175
or they can lock
everything down,

00:17:38.175 --> 00:17:42.172
and there's no way for the
user to use these things.

00:17:42.172 --> 00:17:43.880
So I'm going to give
a quick demo of this

00:17:43.880 --> 00:17:48.320
if you wouldn't mind
switching to the WolfVision.

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:51.140
So a good example
of LockTask mode

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:54.020
is devices that are
locked as a kiosk.

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:56.270
So this is something
that could be,

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:59.120
say, like an airport
or a library.

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:02.180
So within Android P we
now have support for this,

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:04.320
and it's actually a very
secure model as well.

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:06.990
So when the user has actually
stopped using the device

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:10.290
or chosen to end their
session, that entire user

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:13.937
is actually deleted, so all
their data goes with them.

00:18:13.937 --> 00:18:15.270
So let me give you a quick demo.

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:16.728
So you can-- here
there's some sort

00:18:16.728 --> 00:18:18.470
of-- there's a
splash screen here.

00:18:18.470 --> 00:18:19.700
This can be fully customized.

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:22.520
I've just given a very simple
example here for Google.

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:24.694
And then off we go
to start a session.

00:18:24.694 --> 00:18:25.610
I've got to apologize.

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:26.870
This isn't the most
glamorous demo.

00:18:26.870 --> 00:18:28.230
I've only got two applications.

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:30.230
But you can imagine that
one of the applications

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:32.930
could be like a
Help application.

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:36.290
And you can see here, this is
just the applications here.

00:18:36.290 --> 00:18:38.660
So when the users actually
finish their session,

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:40.520
they can simply hit
End Session, and that

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:43.154
will clear all of
that user data down,

00:18:43.154 --> 00:18:45.570
or you can wait for the screen
to time out, in which case,

00:18:45.570 --> 00:18:47.160
that process also happens.

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:49.160
And what you can actually
see here when it says,

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:52.570
ending session, is that
process of deleting the user

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:54.980
and actually creating a
new user in the background

00:18:54.980 --> 00:18:57.290
so that when the device
returns to the splash screen

00:18:57.290 --> 00:18:59.960
a new user has been created,
and the next session

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:01.374
can start straightaway.

00:19:01.374 --> 00:19:02.540
So that's the first session.

00:19:03.331 --> 00:19:04.580
Go back to the slides, please.

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:08.045
Thank you.

00:19:08.045 --> 00:19:11.020
Sorry about that.

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:14.790
A similar but slightly
different example of this

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:16.710
is around shared devices.

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:21.150
So you can imagine, perhaps
that I work in a supermarket,

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:23.250
and I work the morning
shift, and I pick up

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:26.460
a device in my morning, and I
use that to check inventory.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:28.470
I'm walking around,
checking the shelves.

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:30.960
I log in with my credentials,
and I have access

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:32.670
to my applications.

00:19:32.670 --> 00:19:34.710
At the end of my
shift, say 2:00 PM,

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:37.392
I put that device back
on the charging cradle,

00:19:37.392 --> 00:19:39.600
and then the person who's
working the afternoon shift

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:42.990
picks up the same device, logs
in with their credentials,

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:45.260
and they're off and
running as well.

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:47.280
The crucial difference
is here is this.

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:50.075
My application data would
remain entirely separate

00:19:50.075 --> 00:19:50.950
from the second user.

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:52.710
They wouldn't be
able to read my email

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:55.080
or even see any of
the kind of inventory

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:58.194
scanning that I've been up to.

00:19:58.194 --> 00:20:00.360
And you know, we provide a
very simple way for users

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:02.799
to log in and out
of this experience.

00:20:02.799 --> 00:20:04.590
So let me give you a
quick example of this.

00:20:09.221 --> 00:20:10.720
So here you can
see, you can imagine

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:13.592
I pick up a device in the
morning, and I log in.

00:20:13.592 --> 00:20:15.220
I've got a very
secure password which

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:16.344
I'm about to show you here.

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:21.280
You can see I've got
my applications here.

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:23.196
So again, very similar
to the earlier example,

00:20:23.196 --> 00:20:25.890
but I've got my selection
of applications here.

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:28.230
You can see, actually,
I've got my email address.

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:31.710
There's an email
saying, Hi, Ian.

00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:33.577
So I could go about,
continue my duties,

00:20:33.577 --> 00:20:35.910
finish whatever I need to do,
and at the end of my shift

00:20:35.910 --> 00:20:38.980
I would just
select, End Session.

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:43.770
Sinduran comes along, he
logs in with his credentials.

00:20:43.770 --> 00:20:48.714
Again, we enforce very
strict password policies.

00:20:48.714 --> 00:20:50.130
So if I log into
Sinduran's email,

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:53.040
you can see there is an
email from Ian saying hello

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:54.600
to Sinduran.

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:57.110
Again, there's no visibility
of each other's data.

00:20:57.110 --> 00:21:00.370
It's effectively two
separate users on the device.

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:02.130
And again, when
Sinduran has completed,

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:03.130
you just simply log out.

00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:10.080
So there's obviously mechanisms
to add new users to the device.

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:12.960
So you can imagine that the
user may pick up the device

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:15.810
and scan a bar code to
log into the device.

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:17.859
We also support signing
in with a Google account,

00:21:17.859 --> 00:21:19.400
and I believe we
also support logging

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:21.350
in with third-party
authentication providers

00:21:21.350 --> 00:21:21.849
as well.

00:21:27.550 --> 00:21:30.730
One of the pieces of
feedback we hear from users

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:31.950
of dedicated devices--

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:35.520
so I mentioned these bar
code-type devices that we see

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:37.160
in use--

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:39.640
is around maintaining
control of their fleets

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:42.670
during business-critical
periods.

00:21:42.670 --> 00:21:44.890
A good example is
the holidays here.

00:21:44.890 --> 00:21:50.020
So the last thing that they want
to happen is for an OTA update

00:21:50.020 --> 00:21:52.360
to come and affect
all the devices, cause

00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:55.000
a bunch of regressions to
their internal applications,

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:58.960
and suddenly on Christmas Eve,
all these applications are

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:01.820
kind of failing, and
people can't make sales.

00:22:01.820 --> 00:22:06.640
So for dedicated devices, we now
introduce this ability for them

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:08.040
to postpone--

00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:09.580
sorry, let me start again.

00:22:09.580 --> 00:22:11.590
For users of dedicated
devices, we've

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:13.480
now given customers
the ability for them

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:16.480
to postpone updates
for up to 90 days.

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:17.410
Why 90 days?

00:22:17.410 --> 00:22:19.800
Why did I pluck that
number out of anywhere?

00:22:19.800 --> 00:22:22.480
It's really for November,
December, January.

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:24.040
That's kind of
that period there,

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:25.500
and we end up with 90 days.

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:28.070
Now you know, I'm sure you've
been to other talks at Google

00:22:28.070 --> 00:22:30.580
today where we talk about
the high investments

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:32.980
we're making in security,
and how we want devices

00:22:32.980 --> 00:22:35.034
to be updated all the time.

00:22:35.034 --> 00:22:36.700
So you can imagine
introducing something

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:39.970
like a 90-day freeze period kind
of goes slightly against that.

00:22:39.970 --> 00:22:41.980
We want devices to be updated.

00:22:41.980 --> 00:22:44.710
So to prevent devices being
locked out indefinitely,

00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:46.090
and hopefully
receiving an update

00:22:46.090 --> 00:22:48.940
once this freeze period
is over, we introduce

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:50.750
a 90-day cooling off period.

00:22:50.750 --> 00:22:53.720
There's a 90-day buffer
between any freeze period.

00:22:53.720 --> 00:22:55.945
So in that time, any
kind of OTA updates

00:22:55.945 --> 00:22:58.450
that are being
postponed or frozen out

00:22:58.450 --> 00:23:00.770
would be pushed down
to device and applied.

00:23:00.770 --> 00:23:03.510
So this avoids the situation
where I've been indefinitely

00:23:03.510 --> 00:23:04.800
locked out of the device.

00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:07.842
That's a state we don't
particularly want to be in.

00:23:07.842 --> 00:23:10.300
And then all the new features,
all the new security updates

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:12.020
that comport with
the platform update

00:23:12.020 --> 00:23:15.464
will be applied during
this cooling off period.

00:23:15.464 --> 00:23:18.100
And then finally, on
security, as I mentioned,

00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:21.910
this is an area where we
will always invest in.

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:24.500
There's a bunch of new
features in this area.

00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:28.060
So let's start with the
separation of the work

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:28.600
challenge.

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:30.016
So you may have
noticed, actually,

00:23:30.016 --> 00:23:32.150
when I re-enabled
the Work profile

00:23:32.150 --> 00:23:35.410
on one of the first demos, I was
prompted to enter a password.

00:23:35.410 --> 00:23:37.480
That's the password
that the administrator

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:41.620
has required that I have to
enter to use the Work profile.

00:23:41.620 --> 00:23:44.890
The administrator can actually
specify some quality arguments

00:23:44.890 --> 00:23:47.410
around that password.

00:23:47.410 --> 00:23:49.720
They can specify that it
has to be a certain length.

00:23:49.720 --> 00:23:52.070
It could actually be
a very long password.

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:54.670
Now what happened in
previous versions of Android

00:23:54.670 --> 00:23:56.530
is if an administrator
has said, right,

00:23:56.530 --> 00:23:58.330
if you want to use
the Work profile,

00:23:58.330 --> 00:24:00.790
you now have to use
a 12-digit password.

00:24:00.790 --> 00:24:04.460
That would then manifest itself
as the device password as well.

00:24:04.460 --> 00:24:06.760
So every time you want
to unlock your phone,

00:24:06.760 --> 00:24:09.040
you're having to enter
this 12-digit password,

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:11.890
and then have the same again
to access your Work profile.

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:14.500
So, not desirable, most people
want to use a fingerprint,

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:16.930
or maybe a short password,
or short pass-in,

00:24:16.930 --> 00:24:19.970
so in P we've kind of split
these things up a little bit.

00:24:19.970 --> 00:24:23.710
So admins can require
different pins and passwords

00:24:23.710 --> 00:24:27.080
for the Work profile versus the
actual unlocking of the device.

00:24:27.080 --> 00:24:28.690
So it's a bit of
a usability win,

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:30.490
but maintaining the
security integrity

00:24:30.490 --> 00:24:33.160
around the work profile.

00:24:33.160 --> 00:24:35.830
Data leakage is a
consistent concern.

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:39.590
We hear from customers who use
Android Enterprise devices,

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:41.530
particularly in
highly-regulated devices

00:24:41.530 --> 00:24:44.090
or highly-secure organizations.

00:24:44.090 --> 00:24:49.360
So we continue to plug gaps as
they come up to our attention.

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:51.730
So what we're actually
introducing in P

00:24:51.730 --> 00:24:54.370
is some additional controls
about what data can be shared

00:24:54.370 --> 00:24:55.750
in and out of the Work profile.

00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:58.530
So many people will be
familiar with the Android share

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:00.349
icon, the Share Intent.

00:25:00.349 --> 00:25:02.140
We're now giving
administrators the ability

00:25:02.140 --> 00:25:06.610
to turn off either one direction
going out of the Work profile,

00:25:06.610 --> 00:25:10.045
or into the Work profile,
or in no directions at all.

00:25:10.045 --> 00:25:12.670
So we've got full flexibility as
to what the direction in which

00:25:12.670 --> 00:25:14.560
data can travel.

00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:18.340
We're also giving
administrators the ability

00:25:18.340 --> 00:25:21.760
to prevent extracting
data via ADB.

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:24.160
So those of you who've used
Android De-bug Bridge, this

00:25:24.160 --> 00:25:27.820
is where you connect your
phone to your host PC,

00:25:27.820 --> 00:25:30.400
and you can use that to
debug certain information

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:31.337
on the phone.

00:25:31.337 --> 00:25:32.920
Administrators flagged
to us that this

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:34.660
could be a potential
security risk,

00:25:34.660 --> 00:25:37.660
so we now have a policy where
extracting data from the Work

00:25:37.660 --> 00:25:41.340
profile by ADB can be disabled.

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:44.740
Verified device
access continues to be

00:25:44.740 --> 00:25:47.800
a key feature around security.

00:25:47.800 --> 00:25:50.380
This is when a device
is perhaps communicating

00:25:50.380 --> 00:25:51.400
with a remote server.

00:25:51.400 --> 00:25:54.350
It has to strongly
attest to its identity.

00:25:54.350 --> 00:25:56.320
So you can imagine
where a device is

00:25:56.320 --> 00:25:58.300
kind of signing into
a remote server,

00:25:58.300 --> 00:26:01.060
it's maybe identifying
itself using its IMEI

00:26:01.060 --> 00:26:03.280
or its serial number
to retrieve whatever

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:05.170
configuration it needs to.

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:07.120
The difficulty is,
IMEI and serial number

00:26:07.120 --> 00:26:09.720
can be quite easily spoofed,
and it could be the case

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:11.220
that the device has
been compromised

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:15.980
and is kind of misrepresenting
itself to the remote server.

00:26:15.980 --> 00:26:18.070
So devices like the
Pixel now support

00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:20.020
hardware-backed authentication.

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:22.700
So we now have APIs in
the platform as well.

00:26:22.700 --> 00:26:26.110
So as well as presenting
my hardware identifiers,

00:26:26.110 --> 00:26:28.990
I can also present a
key or a secure hash

00:26:28.990 --> 00:26:32.140
for those identifiers that
are a strong indication that I

00:26:32.140 --> 00:26:34.540
am the device who
I'm claiming to be.

00:26:34.540 --> 00:26:36.870
So we now have APIs
in the framework that

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:39.430
allow you to use either
Google's keys or your own keys,

00:26:39.430 --> 00:26:43.720
and certificates to
authenticate to remote services.

00:26:43.720 --> 00:26:47.170
And then finally, not the most
sexy feature, I do apologize,

00:26:47.170 --> 00:26:51.610
but when investigating
incidents or security breaches,

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:54.930
administrators like the ability
to interrogate log files

00:26:54.930 --> 00:26:57.940
on the device to understand
what's being going on.

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:01.870
We now have unique identifiers
for both network and security

00:27:01.870 --> 00:27:02.440
logs.

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:04.580
So the reason for that
is that if the logs have

00:27:04.580 --> 00:27:07.820
been somehow tampered with,
or logs have been removed--

00:27:07.820 --> 00:27:09.520
or entries have
been removed, sorry,

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:11.370
they will be easy to
detect because there

00:27:11.370 --> 00:27:14.460
will be some sort of
discrepancy in the unique IDs.

00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:16.360
And then finally we've
expanded the coverage

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:17.790
of security-related actions.

00:27:17.790 --> 00:27:21.400
So there's a sort of additional
level of security actions

00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:24.400
that will now be added
to the log files.

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:28.330
There's even more listing on
security at that short URL.

00:27:28.330 --> 00:27:32.890
I'd encourage you all to
read it at your leisure.

00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:35.740
So in summary, there are
lots of great features coming

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:38.350
in Android P. I've
talked about the three

00:27:38.350 --> 00:27:41.350
key areas our teams work on,
so things with the managed

00:27:41.350 --> 00:27:44.680
devices, the Work profile,
fully managed devices,

00:27:44.680 --> 00:27:46.870
dedicated devices where
we see a lot of innovation

00:27:46.870 --> 00:27:49.660
from partners that you wouldn't
expect, and then finally,

00:27:49.660 --> 00:27:51.820
security.

00:27:51.820 --> 00:27:54.170
Everything I've described
here is public information.

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:57.190
Feel free to take photos
or get started with it.

00:27:57.190 --> 00:27:59.550
It's all part of the
Android P release,

00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:01.749
and there's full documentation
and developer guides

00:28:01.749 --> 00:28:02.790
at Developer.Android.com.

00:28:02.790 --> 00:28:06.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

